.Dd August 28, 2026
.Dt C4C 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm c4ld
.Nd link C4 programs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl #+h
.Op Fl I Ar dir | Fl I Cm -
.Op Fl j Ar n
.Op Fl l Ar c4lib
.Op Fl O
.Op Fl o Ar out
.Op Fl S Ar stdrt
.Op Fl T Ar triple
.Op Fl t Ar type
.Ar objects ...
.Op lld-flags
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility wraps a linker and provides an interface that is more natural to use via
the command line for working with C4 projects. It performs library lookups
according to C4's semantics. Along with the passed objects it
.Pq by default
links the runtime library, and default standard libraries.

Documentation is
.Ud
.Sh EXIT STATUS
On success,
.Nm
exits 0. In case of failure, the following are used:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 100
Invalid invocation. Flags or arguments malformed or missing.
.It 101
Unexpected error. Please file a bug report.
.It 111
General runtime error: syscall failure or LLVM error.
.El
.Sh AUTHORS
.An András Bodor Aq Mt bodand@proton.me
.Sh BUGS
.Em Warning:
this is a massive monstrosity of a binary, including its dependencies. The
toolchain uses LLVM, so that kind of makes sense. Yet, this
.Dq linker
relies on both the clang and lld libraries. This is because the simple act of
doing
.Fl l Ar c
is such an abhorrently ungodly task, that one with a sane mind should not try to
embark on. Natch, if you care not for portability, that eases things. Even then,
however, there are the libc entry points and whatnot, like
.Pa /usr/lib/crt1.o ,
.Pq or is it Pa /usr/lib64/Scrt1.o?
which makes this an effort of exceedingly diminishing returns. So we need to get
clang. And the lld linker to actually link.
